UPDATE companies SET description='The Great Plains Energy Corridor operates from Bismarck, North Dakota, where it was established in 2008 as a division of Bismarck State College. The initiative was launched through the efforts of former U.S. Senator Byron Dorgan with a mandate to develop innovative approaches that connect stakeholders and advance the state''s diverse energy resources.

Instead, it provides information, education, outreach programs, and special events on topics ranging from enhanced geothermal systems and renewable fuels to sustainable development and energy transmission and storage technologies. It leverages Bismarck State College''s nationally recognized energy education programs, which deliver state-of-the-art training to current and future industry employees. The office is housed in the National Energy Center of Excellence (NECE) building on the college campus, a facility dedicated in September 2008 that incorporates a ground-source geothermal heat pump system, LEED-specification lighting, a total energy heat recovery system, and Flexcrete — a construction material made with fly ash from coal combustion.

The organization serves partners across government, education, and the private sector, helping to secure federal funds and other resources for energy-related projects that benefit North Dakota. Its audience includes energy industry employers, policy makers, educators, and students pursuing energy technology careers. The Corridor also produces an annual report highlighting North Dakota energy industry statistics and hosts the EmPowerND Energy Conference, though the Fall 2023 edition was canceled.

The Corridor was created specifically to foster collaboration that accelerates energy development in North Dakota, positioning the state as a vibrant and innovative energy hub. Its differentiation lies in its direct integration with a college energy curriculum, its physical presence in a high-efficiency demonstration building, and its role as a conduit for federal energy funding into regional projects.', certifications=ARRAY['LEED']::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Energy industry stakeholder convening", "Energy education and workforce training coordination", "Outreach program development", "Special event management", "Federal grant facilitation for energy projects", "Energy industry data compilation and reporting"], "industries": ["Energy", "Education", "Government", "Renewable energy", "Geothermal energy", "Sustainable development", "Energy transmission and storage"], "materials": ["Fly ash", "Flexcrete"], "equipment": ["Ground-source geothermal heat pump system", "Total energy heat recovery system", "Flexcrete construction material"], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Division of Bismarck State College with integrated energy education programs", "Operates from LEED-specification National Energy Center of Excellence building", "Facilitates federal funding for North Dakota energy projects", "Publishes annual North Dakota energy industry statistical report", "Hosts EmPowerND Energy Conference for industry stakeholders"], "facts_used": ["Founded 2008 at Bismarck State College -> /about", "Created by former U.S. Senator Byron Dorgan -> /about", "Division of Bismarck State College -> /about, /", "Headquartered in Bismarck, North Dakota -> db, /about", "Office in National Energy Center of Excellence (NECE) building -> /about", "NECE building dedicated September 2008 -> /about", "Building features: geothermal heat pump, LEED lighting, energy heat recovery, Flexcrete -> /about", "Works with BSC energy education programs -> /about", "Provides information, education, outreach, special events -> /about", "Partners: government, education, private sector -> /about", "Publishes annual energy report -> /, /about", "Hosts EmPowerND Energy Conference -> /, /about", "Fall 2023 conference canceled -> /, /about", "Department of Energy Award DE-OE0000079 -> /, /about", "LEED certification mentioned for building -> /about"], "company_type": "Other", "makes_products": "no", "type_evidence": "The Great Plains Energy Corridor was created at Bismarck State College in 2008 through the efforts of former U.S. Senator Byron Dorgan. The Corridor was tasked with providing innovative approaches that bring people together to advance the development of North Dakota''s abundant and diverse energy resources.", "founded_year": "2008", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='great-plains-energy-corridor' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Green Bay Plastics, Inc. was incorporated in 1957 in Green Bay, Wisconsin, initially as part of Mosinee Paper Corporation. In 1989, J. Kevin Hogan and his wife JoAnn led a buy-out, returning the company to family ownership. Today it remains family-owned and operated, with Michael Hogan as president and general manager, Patrick Hogan as vice president, and Kathleen Hogan-Lesatz as controller and secretary-treasurer.

The company specializes in custom blow molding, producing plastic bottles, jars, canisters, containers, and wheels. They design and build their own manufacturing machinery, auxiliary equipment, and tooling in-house. Production runs range from a few thousand units to mass-production volumes. Additional services include labeling, painting, and special packaging.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Custom blow molding", "Labeling", "Packaging", "Painting", "Tooling design and fabrication", "Machinery design and fabrication"], "industries": ["Food and beverage", "Lubricants", "Personal care", "Dietary supplements", "Medical and healthcare", "Household products", "Sporting goods", "Toy components", "Industrial applications"], "materials": ["HDPE"], "equipment": ["Custom manufacturing machinery", "Auxiliary equipment", "Tooling"], "volume": "Production run sizes that can range from a few thousand to mass-production capabilities", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Family-owned and operated since 1957", "Design and build own manufacturing machinery and auxiliary equipment", "Production runs from a few thousand to mass-production volumes", "Custom labeling and packaging services", "In-house tooling and machinery design"], "facts_used": ["Founded 1957 -> /about-us", "Family-owned since 1957 -> /", "Location Green Bay, Wisconsin -> db", "Custom blow molding -> /", "Labeling and packaging services -> /", "Design and build own machinery -> /", "Products: bottles, jars, containers, wheels -> /", "Industries: food, lubricants, toy components, industrial, medical -> / and /about-us", "Production runs few thousand to mass-production -> /", "Materials: HDPE -> /", "Management: Michael Hogan president, Patrick Hogan VP, Kathleen Hogan-Lesatz controller -> /about-us", "Buy-out from Mosinee Paper Corporation 1989 -> /about-us"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "Custom manufacturer of plastic bottles, jars, and industrial components and products.", "founded_year": "1957", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": ["Family-owned"]}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='green-bay-plastics' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Green Brook Electronics was founded in 1974 in Green Brook, New Jersey, and has operated as a supplier of electronic components and instrumentation for five decades. In 2018, the company relocated to a facility in North Plainfield, New Jersey, that is more than seven times larger than its original store, expanding its capacity for inventory and services.

The business stocks over 300,000 distinct line items across multiple warehouse locations, ranging from semiconductors, relays, capacitors, and resistors to wire, cable, fuses, and audio/video cables. Beyond distribution, Green Brook Electronics operates a retail store open to the public, a rental center for test equipment, a repair center capable of board-level and surface-mount work on items such as medical devices and tube amplifiers, a movie prop center offering vintage test equipment and computers for film productions, a technology studio providing soldering and Arduino lessons, a vinyl record shop, and a component identification service called Component Eye-D.

Customers include large technology firms, industrial buyers, hobbyists, film production companies, and students seeking hands-on electronics training. The company ships worldwide and emphasizes its ability to source obsolete or long-lead-time parts.

Green Brook Electronics positions itself as a solution provider rather than a simple distributor, highlighting its repair expertise, obsolete-part sourcing, and the breadth of services — from rentals to education — under one roof.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Electronic component distribution", "Obsolete component sourcing", "Electronic repair (board-level, surface-mount, cable, medical equipment, tube amplifiers)", "Equipment rental", "Component identification", "Technology training (soldering, Arduino, LEDs)", "Movie prop rental (vintage test equipment, scientific instruments, vintage computers)", "Vinyl record sales", "Technical book sales", "Worldwide shipping", "Industrial sales", "Retail sales"], "industries": ["Technology", "Industrial", "Entertainment", "Education", "Hobbyist"], "materials": [], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Over 300,000 line items stocked across multiple warehouses", "Expertise in sourcing obsolete and long-lead-time components", "Repair services for board-level, surface-mount, medical equipment, and vintage audio", "Equipment rental center with frequently updated inventory", "Component identification service (Component Eye-D)"], "facts_used": ["Founded in 1974 -> db", "Headquarters in North Plainfield, New Jersey -> db", "Originally located in Green Brook, New Jersey -> /about-us", "Stock over 300,000 line items -> /, /about-us", "Multiple warehouse locations -> /", "Retail store open to public -> /", "Rental center for equipment -> /", "Repair center for board-level, surface-mount, medical equipment, tube amps -> /", "Movie prop center with vintage test equipment, scientific instruments, vintage computers -> /", "Technology studios offering soldering, Arduino, music lessons, LEDs -> /", "Vinyl center for vintage records and record players -> /", "Component Eye-D identification service -> /", "Worldwide shipping -> /about-us", "Industrial sales -> /about-us"